    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for my circuits final and i'm a bit stuck. can u explain how a capacitor acts differently when it's in a DC circuit vs an AC circuit? also whats the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor initially blocks the current as it charges, but eventually acts as an open circuit once fully charged. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the capacitor continuously charges and discharges, allowing AC current to pass through it. Capacitive reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$), which is the opposition a capacitor offers to AC, is calculated using the formula $\\text{X}_\\text{C} = 1 / (2\\pi fC)$, where $f$ is frequency and $C$ is capacitance in Farads.",
