    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i get the displacement current thing. so if the capacitor is basically just reacting to the change in voltage, does the frequency of the AC have a massive impact on how much \"opposition\" there is? like if i turn the frequency way up, does the capacitor eventually stop acting like a resistor and start acting more like a plain old wire?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, frequency has a massive impact on capacitive reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$). As the frequency ($f$) increases, the capacitive reactance decreases ($\\text{X}_\\text{C} \\propto 1/f$). At extremely high frequencies, $\\text{X}_\\text{C}$ approaches zero, meaning the capacitor offers very little opposition and begins to behave much more like a short circuit or a low-impedance wire. This highlights that the capacitor's impedance is highly frequency-dependent.",
      "source": "target",
